{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":75802,"ns":0,"title":"Consistency","extract":"In deductive logic, a consistent theory is one that does not lead to a logical contradiction. A theory \n  \n    \n      \n        T\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle T}\n  \n is consistent if there is no formula \n  \n    \n      \n        φ\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\varphi }\n  \n such that both \n  \n    \n      \n        φ\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\varphi }\n  \n and its negation \n  \n    \n      \n        ¬\n        φ\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\lnot \\varphi }\n  \n are elements of the set of consequences of \n  \n    \n      \n        T\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle T}\n  \n.","description":"Non-contradiction of a theory","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}